Indexes are specialized data structures that optimize data retrieval speed in Mongodb.
Indexes are main kam ye hai k hamari query k tasks ko speed up kr dta ha or u can also say that hamary task ki speed ko increase kr dta ha.Indexes can speed up our tasks by creating a indexes.Indexes basically data ko smaller parts mei aisy store krta ha jis sy data ko search krny me aur zyada easy hu. By using this,hamary task ki speed boht achi hu jati ha agr phly isy 10 sec lgty thy tu ab isy use kr k 5 ya 3 second lg jaty ha.

Imp:
Indexes are separate from collections and multiple indexes can exist per collection.

Benefits
Faster speed
Effecient sorting
Improved aggregation
Make a Easy searching because of indexing on multiple fields

=>How Does an Index Work Behind the Scenes?

When you create an index on a field (say title), MongoDB creates a B-tree (balanced tree) structure.

This B-tree stores sorted values of that field and pointers (references) to the actual documents in the collection.

👉 Example:
Suppose your collection has:

{ "_id": 1, "title": "Dog" }
{ "_id": 2, "title": "Cat" }
{ "_id": 3, "title": "Apple" }


If you create an index on title:

db.books.createIndex({ title: 1 })


MongoDB internally creates something like:

Index: title_1
----------------
"Apple" → points to _id: 3
"Cat"   → points to _id: 2
"Dog"   → points to _id: 1


This index structure is stored efficiently (like another hidden data file).

When you query db.books.find({ title: "Cat" }), MongoDB doesn’t scan all documents.

Instead, it goes straight to "Cat" in the B-tree and fetches _id: 2.

3. Does It Create Another Collection?

No, not a collection you can see.

Indexes are stored in special internal data files alongside your collection data, but not as a separate collection.

That’s why you don’t see them in show collections.

Types of indexes:
=====>Single field index
Created on one field.

Can be ascending (1) or descending (-1).

👉 Example:

db.users.createIndex({ age: 1 })


Queries like db.users.find({ age: 25 }) will be much faster.

===>Compound index
Index on multiple fields together.

Order of fields matters!

👉 Example:

db.users.createIndex({ lastName: 1, firstName: 1 })


Optimizes queries like:

db.users.find({ lastName: "Smith", firstName: "John" })


But only efficient if you query starting with the left-most field (here lastName).Indexes will work fine when You  give first field or first and last field together but not on alone second field(here firstName).

====>Multikey index
Created when you index an array field.

MongoDB indexes each element of the array.

👉 Example:

db.products.createIndex({ tags: 1 })


For this document:

{ "tags": ["electronics", "phone", "android"] }


The index stores each value separately → electronics, phone, android.
db.articles.find().sort({ tags: 1 })


Where not to use Indexes:
Do not create index of that particular field which is rarely used
do not Use indexes on small collections and on a very large collections.

